WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.150
Eight healthy people.

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:02.200
Twelve hospitals.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.225
All admitted.

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:06.826
That's what Stanford psychologist
David Rosenhan reported

00:00:06.836 --> 00:00:08.368
in 1973.

00:00:08.378 --> 00:00:09.927
He said each told doctors the same

00:00:09.937 --> 00:00:10.662
thing:

00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:13.230
a voice was saying empty, hollow, thud.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.718
That was the only symptom.

00:00:14.728 --> 00:00:16.958
Once inside, they acted normally.

00:00:16.968 --> 00:00:19.364
Most were labelled with
schizophrenia, and kept

00:00:19.374 --> 00:00:21.271
for days, some for weeks.

00:00:21.281 --> 00:00:23.060
His paper in the journal Science shook

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.061
psychiatry.

00:00:24.071 --> 00:00:27.774
Then journalist Susannah Cahalan
investigated, digging through

00:00:27.784 --> 00:00:29.887
his notes and files for her 2019

00:00:29.897 --> 00:00:30.474
book.

00:00:30.484 --> 00:00:32.611
Of the other seven volunteers, she could

00:00:32.621 --> 00:00:34.148
identify only one.

00:00:34.158 --> 00:00:35.543
And she found his account of his

00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:38.541
own hospital stay differed
from the records.

00:00:38.551 --> 00:00:39.658
Her conclusion:

00:00:39.668 --> 00:00:41.971
he misrepresented, and
probably made up, some

00:00:41.981 --> 00:00:42.834
of the data.

00:00:42.844 --> 00:00:45.068
Even so, she believes it exposed something

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:45.878
real.
